<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Tahir al-Jannabi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">906–944</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Tahir al-Jannabi was ruler of the Qarmatian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> from 923 until his death in 944. He led raids on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> and the pilgrim caravans, threatened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, and in January 930 sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> during the pilgrimage, killing many pilgrims and carrying off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-stone">Black Stone</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a> to al-Ahsa, where it stayed until 951. In 931 he briefly hailed a young Persian as the awaited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>, with disastrous results.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abu Tahir Sulayman was an Arabized Persian, born in June or July 906 at Ganaveh on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>. His father, Abu Sa'id, a tribal leader taught by Hamdan Qarmat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, had begun the militarization of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarmatians">Qarmatians</a>.</p><p>Most Arabic sources agree that Abu Sa'id named his eldest son, Abu'l-Qasim Sa'id, as heir and that Abu Tahir revolted against him and usurped power. Another tradition, reported by the Kufan anti-Isma'ili polemicist Ibn Rizam, holds that Sa'id was only a regent and handed over power in 917/918, when his brother was barely ten. It is likely that power was nominally shared among all the sons, with Abu Tahir dominant. Abu'l-Qasim was not executed and lived until 972.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 926 he led his army as far north as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> and made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> pay large sums for him to leave. On his return he built palaces at Ahsa and declared it his permanent capital. In 928 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> al-Muqtadir sent against him the generals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-abil-saj">Yusuf ibn Abi'l-Saj</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munis-al-muzaffar">Mu'nis al-Muzaffar</a> and Harun, who were beaten and driven back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>.</p><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> in 930 he was at first refused entry, invoked the right of all Muslims to enter and swore that he came in peace. Once inside, his men rode into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masjid-al-haram">Masjid al-Haram</a>, massacred the pilgrims, set fire to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a> and threw bodies into the Zamzam Well. The attack was believed to be meant to prompt the appearance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> and followed millenarian excitement over the conjunction of Saturn and Jupiter in 928.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> he recognised in 931, Abu'l-Fadl al-Isfahani, a prisoner who claimed descent from the Sasanian kings, restored the veneration of fire, burned religious books and had members of Bahrayn's notable families executed. In one account Abu Tahir's mother exposed him and his brother Sa'id killed him after eighty days; in others Abu Tahir denounced him as a false Mahdi and had him executed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Abu Tahir took back control of the state and resumed his attacks on pilgrims crossing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. He rejected the attempts of both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> to persuade him to return the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-stone">Black Stone</a>. He died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> in 944, aged about 38, and was succeeded by his three surviving sons and his nephews. According to the historian al-Juwayni the stone came back in 951, thrown in a sack into the Great Mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a> with a note: &quot;By command we took it, and by command we have brought it back.&quot; Its removal had broken it into seven pieces.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abu Tahir al-Jannabi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under <b>Abu Tahir</b> the Qarmatians raided Basra and Kufa, attacked pilgrim caravans and in 930 sacked Mecca, carrying the Black Stone to al-Ahsa until 951.</blockquote><a href="/state/qarmatians-of-bahrain">Qarmatians of Bahrain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Eastern Arabia. Died in Eastern Arabia. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
